<title>USA: Cool temperatures, no rain slow Fla citrus growth.</title>
<headline>Cool temperatures, no rain slow Fla citrus growth.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1996-10-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Cool temperatures and no measurable rain slowed new citrus growth for the week ended Oct 27, the Florida Agricultural Statistics Service (FASS), said.</p>
<p>Temperatures averaged two degrees to five degrees below normal in most inland areas, with coastal averages normal to one degree below. Lows hit the 40s and 50s (Faherenheit) in most northern, central and southern areas. Highs reached the mid to upper 80s, FASS said.</p>
<p>Additionally, &quot;harvest of the early fruit is gaining momentum as more groves are passing minimum maturity tests,&quot; FASS said.</p>
<p>Growers continue to apply a few sprays and fertilizers.</p>
<p>-- New York Commodity Desk, 212-859-1640</p>
